{"name":"Aspen Valley","hex":"#687f7a","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aspen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to the aspen tree.","Tremulous, trembling."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A poplar tree, especially of section Populus sect. Populus, of medium-size trees with thin, straight trunks of a greenish-white color.","The wood of such a tree; usually pale, lightweight and soft."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aspen"},{"term":"valley","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elongated depression cast between hills or mountains, often with a river flowing through it.","An area which drains itself into a river."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form the shape of a valley."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/valley"}],"entries":[{"title":"Roaring Fork Valley","blocks":[{"text":"The Roaring Fork Valley is a geographical region in western Colorado in the United States. The Roaring Fork Valley is one of the most affluent regions in Colorado and the U.S. as well as one of the most populous and economically vital areas of the Colorado Western Slope. The Valley is defined by the valley of the Roaring Fork River and its tributaries, including the Crystal and Fryingpan River. It includes the communities of Aspen, Snowmass Village, Basalt, Carbondale, and Glenwood Springs. Mount Sopris and the Roaring Fork River serve as symbols of the Roaring Fork Valley."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Downtown_of_Aspen%2C_Colorado.jpg/330px-Downtown_of_Aspen%2C_Colorado.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roaring_Fork_Valley"},{"title":"Aspen","blocks":[{"text":"Aspen is a common name for certain tree species in the Populus sect. Populus, of the Populus (poplar) genus.\nAspens were referred to as \"asps\" in earlier forms of English."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Snowbowlaspens.jpg/330px-Snowbowlaspens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aspen"},{"title":"Valley","blocks":[{"text":"A valley is an elongated low area often running between hills or mountains and typically containing a river or stream running from one end to the other. Most valleys are formed by erosion of the land surface by rivers or streams over a very long period. Some valleys are formed through erosion by glacial ice. These glaciers may remain present in valleys in high mountains or polar areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Salta-VallesCalchaquies-P3140151.JPG/330px-Salta-VallesCalchaquies-P3140151.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valley"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Mount_Sopris.JPG/500px-Mount_Sopris.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Mount_Sopris.JPG/1280px-Mount_Sopris.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Mount_Sopris.JPG/500px-Mount_Sopris.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMount_Sopris.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Glenwood_springs_co.jpg/500px-Glenwood_springs_co.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Glenwood_springs_co.jpg/1280px-Glenwood_springs_co.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Glenwood_springs_co.jpg/500px-Glenwood_springs_co.jpg?utm_source=en.wiki